===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of [[deflecting]] or something [[deflected]].
#: {{ux|en|Russell's goalbound shot took a '''deflection''' off a defender and went out for a corner.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1918|author=H. G. Wells|title=Joan and Peter
|passage=The next morning Oswald stopped short in the middle of his shaving, which in his case involved the most tortuous '''deflections''' and grimacings.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=March|author=H. A. Vallance|title=On Foot Across the Forth Bridge|journal=Railway Magazine|page=148|text=The structure was examined and passed by the Board of Trade in February, 1890, after it had been tested with two trains of 50 loaded wagons, each hauled by two engines, and pushed by a third. The trains ran side by side, and their combined weight, including the six engines, was 1,800 tons. The '''deflections''' of the girders were precisely in accordance with the calculations of the engineers.}}
# {{cap|deviation}} (of a [[needle]] or other [[indicator]] or [[mechanism]]) from a previous [[position]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|month=June|author=Presidency of Civil Aviation of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia|title=McDonnell-Douglas DC-8-61, C-GMXQ, accident at King Abdulaziz International Airport, Jeddah, Saudi Arabia, on 11 July 1991|chapter=2.7.2 Take-Off|url=https://reports.aviation-safety.net/1991/19910711-0_DC86_C-GMXQ.pdf|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220514032252/https://reports.aviation-safety.net/1991/19910711-0_DC86_C-GMXQ.pdf|archivedate=14 May 2022|accessdate=1 August 2024|page=|text=At about the time that #1 tyre failed, the Flight Data Recorder shows right rudder '''deflection'''. This may have been applied to correct a yaw to the left. (Traces of the left main gear on the runway show that the nose wheels were to the right of the centreline and never crossed it.) About 500 metres from the threshold, the inner wheels of the left main gear bogie had closed from about 2 1⁄2 metres to about one metre to the left of the centreline; this may have been because of over-correction for a tendency to yaw to the left or weather-cocking because of the surface wind. Whatever the reason, the actual deviation would probably have been considered insignificant to the cockpit crew.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} The act of [[refuse|refusing]] to [[address]] something (questions, criticism, etc.).
# {{cap|bending}} or [[deformation]] (of a [[tire]] or [[component]] thereof) under [[load]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|month=June|author=Presidency of Civil Aviation of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia|title=McDonnell-Douglas DC-8-61, C-GMXQ, accident at King Abdulaziz International Airport, Jeddah, Saudi Arabia, on 11 July 1991|chapter=2.4 Tyre Failure|url=https://reports.aviation-safety.net/1991/19910711-0_DC86_C-GMXQ.pdf|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220514032252/https://reports.aviation-safety.net/1991/19910711-0_DC86_C-GMXQ.pdf|archivedate=14 May 2022|accessdate=1 August 2024|page=|text=Tyre design '''deflection''' profile under load is about a one-third reduction in tyre section radius. If the tyre is overloaded, over-'''deflection''' will occur. Over-'''deflection''' results in a larger heat increase than would be expected from under-inflation. If one of a pair of tyres on the same axle is under-inflated, the other tyre may be overloaded.}}

